-- =====================================================================
-- DEFENSE_TEST_DATA.sql
-- Synthetic test data: "Typhoon Binitayan Defense Demonstration 2026"
-- Web-Based Post Disaster Monitoring and Relief Allocation with Decision
-- Support System for Barangay Binitayan — Phase 19 schema (barangay_disaster_db_FINAL.sql)
--
-- SYNTHETIC TEST DATA ONLY. All names and 0900 numbers are fictional.
-- Every record is marked [DEFENSE TEST] (or uses a binitayan.test e-mail) so
-- DEFENSE_TEST_DATA_CLEANUP.sql can remove exactly these records.
--
-- HOW TO IMPORT: phpMyAdmin -> select the (defense/test) database -> Import
--   -> this file -> Go. See DEFENSE_TEST_DATA_IMPORT_GUIDE.md.
--
-- WHAT THIS FILE DOES
--   * INSERT statements only (plus SET NAMES, SET time_zone, START TRANSACTION, COMMIT and
--     user variables that carry new AUTO_INCREMENT ids to child rows).
--   * No CREATE / ALTER / DROP / TRUNCATE / DELETE. Foreign key checks stay ON.
--   * No primary keys are hard-coded: every id comes from LAST_INSERT_ID(),
--     so existing records are never overwritten.
--   * No DSS scores, ranks or recommendations are stored (the schema has no
--     such columns): the application calculates them when a page is opened.
--   * No fake audit logs, SMS logs, notifications or distributed records.
--
-- DUPLICATE PROTECTION: users.email is UNIQUE. If any test account already
-- exists, the first INSERT stops with "Duplicate entry ... for key 'email'"
-- and NOTHING is saved (the transaction is never committed). Run
-- DEFENSE_TEST_DATA_CLEANUP.sql first, then import again.
--
-- Password for all 10 test accounts: see DEFENSE_TEST_DATA_IMPORT_GUIDE.md.
-- Stored here ONLY as bcrypt hashes made with PHP password_hash() (PASSWORD_DEFAULT),
-- the same call as backend/users/add_user.php; login uses password_verify().
-- Generated by database/defense_demo/build_defense_test_data_sql.php
-- =====================================================================

SET NAMES utf8mb4;
-- Same session time zone the application uses (DB_TIME_ZONE '+08:00', Philippine
-- time), so the scenario timestamps below show correctly on any server.
SET time_zone = '+08:00';
START TRANSACTION;

